body:
Choose Fresh Passport only if you have never held an ordinary passport before. If you held an ordinary passport at any time, even one that expired long ago or was lost, you apply under Re-issue. Holding an official or diplomatic passport does not count: if you never had an ordinary passport, you still apply as fresh for an ordinary one.

Re-issue covers these situations:
- your passport has expired, or will expire within the next 10 years;
- your passport expired more than 3 years ago (fresh police verification will be started in this case);
- the pages are used up (extra pages are never added to an old booklet);
- the passport is damaged or lost;
- you want to change personal details such as name, address, photo, signature, date or place of birth, spouse or parent name, or remove ECR status.

A re-issued passport is a new booklet with a new passport number and fresh validity. The portal does not issue a 'duplicate' with the old number.

If you applied earlier but the passport was never issued and you have never held one, apply as fresh again. On the Previous Passport page, give the old file number.

Re-issue within 3 years after expiry normally needs no fresh police verification, provided the earlier police report was clear and the system shows no adverse entry.

You need proof of your present address only. It does not matter how long you have lived there. You must still list in the form every address where you stayed during the previous year.

Accepted documents (any one):
- water, electricity, gas, or telephone bill (landline or postpaid mobile);
- Income Tax assessment order;
- Election Commission photo ID (voter ID);
- certificate on letterhead from an employer that is a reputed public limited company, with seal (computer printouts are not accepted);
- spouse's passport copy (first and last page, naming you as spouse), if your address matches the address in it;
- for minors, a parent's passport copy (first and last page);
- Aadhaar letter, card or e-Aadhaar, subject to successful validation with UIDAI;
- rent agreement;
- photo passbook of a running account with a scheduled public sector bank, a scheduled private Indian bank or a regional rural bank.

Bank statements are not accepted; only a photo passbook is. Any other document on the Tatkaal acceptable-documents list may also serve, if it shows the same present address as your form.

The official guidance says that furnishing Aadhaar speeds up processing. For re-issue, address proof is needed only if your address differs from the old passport. For minors, address proof in a parent's name can be used.

Only the present address is printed in the passport, never the permanent one.

ECR means Emigration Check Required. ECR passport holders need clearance from the Protector of Emigrants before they leave India to take up a job in any of these 18 countries: UAE, Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Oman, Kuwait, Bahrain, Malaysia, Libya, Jordan, Yemen, Sudan, South Sudan, Afghanistan, Indonesia, Syria, Lebanon, Thailand and Iraq. Without clearance they are stopped at the port of exit. The booklet's version of the list names Brunei in place of South Sudan.

Travel for other purposes, such as tourism, does not need emigration clearance. ECR holders who already hold an employment visa and are going back after a short visit to India do not need clearance either.

Where to look: ECR status is printed at the top of the last page. If nothing is printed there, the passport is Non-ECR. Stamps are no longer used.

You qualify for Non-ECR, with proof, if you are:
- a matriculate (10th pass) or more qualified;
- above 50;
- a gazetted government servant or their family;
- an income-tax payer or their family (NIL returns do not count);
- a professional degree holder;
- a holder of a recognised diploma or nursing qualification;
- someone who has lived abroad more than 3 years, or their spouse;
- a minor taking a short-validity passport;
- among certain other listed categories.

To remove ECR, apply for re-issue with reason 'Deletion of ECR'. Bring your old passport and Non-ECR proof, for example your matriculation certificate. A provisional certificate is accepted if you have the mark sheets for all years.

If your passport is lost or stolen, report it straight away to the nearest police station. Also inform the Passport Office, or the Indian Mission if you are abroad. You cannot report a loss through the call centre.

To replace it, apply for re-issue with the reason 'Lost' or 'Damaged'. You will not get a duplicate with the same number. The new passport has a new number and fresh validity.

Documents for a lost passport usually include:
- proof of present address;
- proof of date of birth;
- an Annexure F affidavit saying how and where it was lost or damaged;
- the original police report;
- a self-attested copy of the first two and last two pages of the old passport, if you have one.
A photocopy is not compulsory, but you must give the old passport number, issue and expiry dates and place of issue. If you do not have them, visit your nearest Passport Office, or the Indian Mission if abroad. Re-issue after a loss needs police verification.

Damage comes in two kinds. 'Damaged' means the passport number and name are readable and the photo is intact; it can be re-issued under Tatkaal. 'Damaged beyond recognition' cannot use Tatkaal. Lost or stolen passports are also excluded from Tatkaal.

Fee as of October 2026: Rs 5,000 (36 pages) or Rs 6,000 (60 pages), plus Rs 2,500 if Tatkaal applies.

If you lose your passport abroad and must return urgently, the Indian Mission can generally issue an Emergency Certificate after verifying your nationality.

Details printed in a passport cannot be edited. To change any of them, apply for Re-issue, choose 'Change in existing personal particulars' and tick what is changing. This covers:
- name (given name or surname);
- address;
- photo or appearance;
- signature;
- spouse name (add, change or delete);
- date or place of birth;
- gender;
- father's or mother's name;
- ECR status.

Useful rules:
- Surname change after marriage or divorce (women applicants): no extra document is required beyond the standard list.
- Address change: bring your old passport and a self-attested copy of its first two and last two pages, plus proof of the new present address.
- Any name change, as well as changes to date or place of birth, gender, appearance, signature, or parent or spouse names, cannot use Tatkaal.
- Date or place of birth, gender and parent-name changes are examined case by case. More documents may be sought, and the request may be refused.
- Do not travel under a previous name.
- Address change during processing: the passport is dispatched to the present address in the form, so contact the Passport Office concerned.

Printing errors: if the printed passport does not match what you submitted, return it to the Regional Passport Office for rectification. If approved, a new booklet with a new number is issued; any fee depends on the case. Check your details carefully at Counter A, because errors in data you entered yourself are your responsibility.
